course: physics
lesson: 4
title: Pushes and pulls: what makes things move and stop
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A push or a pull is called a force, and a force is what starts things moving, stops them, speeds them up or turns them.
explain: Kick a football and your foot pushes it for a split second; the harder the kick, the faster it flies.
explain: Nothing stops "by itself": a rolling ball slows down because the grass and the air keep pushing against it a little.
explain: Forces come in pairs: stand on a skateboard and throw a heavy ball forward, and the ball pushes you backwards just as hard.
explain: When two forces are equal and opposite, as in a tug of war where nobody wins, they cancel and nothing changes.
fact: A force is a push or a pull, and it is what changes how something moves.
fact: A harder kick gives a bigger push and a faster ball.
fact: A rolling ball slows down because the grass and the air push against it; in empty space, with nothing in the way, it would roll on for ever.
fact: A toy car rolls further on a smooth floor than on a carpet, because the carpet's fibres catch the wheels and push back much more.
fact: In a tug of war where nobody moves, both teams pull just as hard in opposite directions, so the forces cancel.
fact: A book on a table stays put because gravity pulls it down and the table, squashed a tiny bit like a very stiff spring, pushes it up just as hard.
fact: Two people pushing a heavy wardrobe add their pushes together, and once it is sliding it is a bit easier to keep it going, because the floor grips it a bit less once it moves.
fact: The more mass something has, the harder it is to start, stop or turn, so a full trolley is harder to push and to stop than an empty one.
fact: Every push comes in a pair: when you push something, it pushes you back just as hard.
fact: When you jump off a small boat, your legs push the boat backwards, so it drifts away; step off carefully with a hand on the jetty.
fact: A balloon let go without its knot squeezes air out of its neck, and the air rushing out pushes the balloon the other way.
fact: Rockets work in the same way, pushing hot gas out of the back so that the gas pushes them forward.
exercise: What made the football start moving? | Your foot pushed it for a split second | The grass under it tipped it forwards a bit | The ball wanted to move | The wind pulled it along
exercise: What is a force? | A push or a pull | A kind of speed | A kind of weight | The size of a thing
exercise: Why does a rolling ball slow down and stop? | The grass and the air keep pushing against it | It gets tired and runs out of moving | Gravity pulls it backwards | Balls always stop by themselves after about ten metres
exercise: Why does a toy car roll further on a smooth floor than on a carpet? | The carpet pushes back on the wheels much more | The smooth floor pulls the car forward a little | The car is lighter on a smooth floor | Carpets make the car heavier
exercise: In a tug of war, why can the rope stay still while everyone pulls hard? | Both teams pull equally hard in opposite directions | Nobody is really pulling at all | The rope is too heavy to move | The ground holds the rope down
exercise: What happens the moment one team pulls a little harder? | The rope starts moving their way | The rope snaps in the middle | Nothing, the rope stays still | The rope moves towards the weaker team
exercise: A book lies on a table. Is the table pushing the book? | Yes, it pushes up as hard as gravity pulls down | No, tables cannot push anything | No, the book just rests there with no forces at all | Yes, but much harder than gravity
exercise: Why does a wardrobe slide once a friend helps you push? | Your two pushes add into one bigger push | Your friend somehow makes the wardrobe lighter | Two people make the floor smoother | The wardrobe gets tired of resisting
exercise: Why is it a bit easier to keep the wardrobe moving than to start it? | Once it slides, the floor grips it a bit less | It gets lighter once it is moving along the floor | The floor tilts as you push | The wardrobe starts helping you
exercise: Why is a full trolley harder to push than an empty one? | More mass needs a bigger push to speed up | The wheels are smaller when it is full | Full trolleys always have a stuck wheel or two | Food pulls the trolley backwards
exercise: Why is a full trolley rolling down a slope hard to stop? | The more mass, the harder it is to stop | Slopes make trolleys lighter | The wheels lock up whenever they are on slopes | Trolleys only stop on flat ground
exercise: Why do you roll backwards on a skateboard when you throw a heavy ball forward? | The ball pushes you back as you push it | The air pushes you back | The skateboard rolls backwards on its own | Gravity pulls you backwards
exercise: Why does a small boat drift away when you jump off it? | Your legs push the boat back as they push you forward | The water pulls the boat out | The wind blows it out every time | The boat is lighter without you
exercise: What pushes a balloon forward when you let it go without a knot? | The air rushing out of its neck | The air in the room pulls it along | The rubber pulls itself along like a worm | Gravity pushes it sideways across the room
exercise: How does a rocket move forward? | It pushes gas out of the back, and the gas pushes it | It pushes against the air in front | It is pulled by the Moon | It floats up because it is light
exercise: In empty space, with nothing in the way, what would a rolling ball do? | Roll on for ever | Slow down and stop | Speed up by itself | Roll back to where it started
test: My mate says things just stop on their own when they run out of push. right? | No, something always pushes against them | Yes, every push runs out in time and then stops | Yes, things get tired | No, things speed up on their own
test: In a cartoon a man stands in a rowing boat and blows into its sail to move it. What is wrong? | His breath pushes the boat back, so it barely moves | Nothing, it works perfectly | Breath is too warm to push | Boats only move with engines
test: You tape a balloon to a straw on a string and let it go. What happens? | It zooms along the string like a little rocket | It stays still on the string | It slides straight back towards you along the string | It floats up off the string
test: shut up and push the trolley, it's full, why's it so hard to stop on the ramp? | More stuff in it means it is harder to stop | Ramps make trolleys heavier the further down they go | The wheels are stuck | Gravity stops on ramps
test: What would happen to a book on a table if the table were taken away? | It would fall, with only gravity left | It would stay floating in the air | It would slowly drift up | It would stay because books have no weight
test: An astronaut in space throws a spanner away. What happens to him? | He moves slowly the other way | He stays perfectly still where he is | He moves off after the spanner | He spins round and round on the spot
test: Which uses the same idea as a rocket? | A balloon whizzing round the room | A ball rolling slowly down a grassy hill | A book lying quite still on a shelf | A leaf drifting down from a tall tree
test: A football player kicks softly, then hard. Which is true? | The harder kick makes a faster ball | The softer kick makes a faster ball | Both kicks make the same speed | Kicks do not change the speed
test: Why should you step off a small boat carefully, with a hand on the jetty? | Your push can send the boat away and you into the water | Jetties are always slippery | Boats sink when you stand up | The jetty pushes you back
test: Two equal teams pull a rope and it does not move. Is there no force at all? | There are big forces, but they cancel | Yes, there is no force on the rope at all | Only the rope itself has any force in it | The ground under them cancels the forces out
test: In a game a car stops dead the moment you let go of the button, even on ice. Is that real? | No, on ice it would slide on a long way | Yes, cars always stop at once | Yes, ice stops cars fastest of all surfaces | No, it would speed up on ice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of the cessation of motion in a rolling ball? | Forces exerted by the ground and the air act in opposition to its motion | The ball exhausts an internal store of motion | The gravitational attraction acts horizontally against it | The ball ceases to move after a fixed interval
exam: It is given that two teams in a tug of war exert equal and opposite forces upon a rope. Determine the consequence for the motion of the rope. | The rope remains at rest | The rope moves towards the stronger team | The rope moves towards the weaker team | The rope is accelerated upwards
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person standing upon a skateboard moves backwards upon throwing a heavy ball forwards. | The ball exerts upon the person a force equal and opposite to that applied | The air in front of the person is compressed | The skateboard possesses a motion of its own | The ground exerts a horizontal force towards the rear
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a laden trolley. | Its greater mass renders it more difficult both to start and to arrest | Its greater mass renders it more easily arrested | Its mass has no bearing upon the force required | Its greater mass causes it to move of its own accord
